    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so the salt basically prevents them from just using a list of pre-calculated hashes (the rainbow tables) to find matches? im still a bit confused about the mechanism though. like, is the salt stored right next to the hash in the database, or is it kept somewhere else to keep it even more secure?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, you've hit the nail on the head regarding rainbow tables! The salt is specifically designed to defeat pre-computed hash lists by ensuring that even if an attacker has a massive table of hashes for common passwords, they can't just look up your password because the \"input\" to the hash function has changed.\n\nAs for the storage mechanism, the salt is definitely stored right next to the hash in the database. In fact, it's stored in plain text alongside the hash. If you were to hide the salt in a separate, secure vault, you'd have a problem: the attacker would need to know the salt to apply it to the password before hashing it. By storing the salt alongside the hash, the attacker can retrieve it, add it to the guessed password, and run the hash function themselves. This turns a pre-computed attack into a slow, individual brute-force attack for each user, which is a massive time sink for the bad guys.\n\nThink of it like a locked box (the database) containing a map (the hash) and a unique instruction manual (the salt) glued to the lid. Even if the map looks the same for two different boxes, the instruction manual ensures the path to the treasure is unique to",
      "source": "target",
